{"id":"CVE-2018-0739","state":"PUBLISHED","assigner":"openssl","published":"2018-03-27T21:00:00.000Z","updated":"2024-09-16T22:35:29.998Z","title":"Constructed ASN.1 types with a recursive definition could exceed the stack","description":"Constructed ASN.1 types with a recursive definition (such as can be found in PKCS7) could eventually exceed the stack given malicious input with excessive recursion. This could result in a Denial Of Service attack. There are no such structures used within SSL/TLS that come from untrusted sources so this is considered safe. Fixed in OpenSSL 1.1.0h (Affected 1.1.0-1.1.0g). Fixed in OpenSSL 1.0.2o (Affected 1.0.2b-1.0.2n).","cwe":["Stack overflow"],"metrics":[{"src":"cna","other":"unknown","content":"{\"lang\": \"eng\", \"url\": \"https://www.openssl.org/policies/secpolicy.html#Moderate\", \"value\": \"Moderate\"}"}],"affected":[{"vendor":"OpenSSL","product":"OpenSSL","versions":["affected Fixed in OpenSSL 1.1.0h (Affected 1.1.0-1.1.0g)","affected Fixed in OpenSSL 1.0.2o (Affected 1.0.2b-1.0.2n)"]}],"references":[{"url":"https://usn.ubuntu.com/3611-2/","tags":["vendor-advisory","x_refsource_UBUNTU"],"name":"USN-3611-2"},{"url":"https://www.debian.org/security/2018/dsa-4158","tags":["vendor-advisory","x_refsource_DEBIAN"],"name":"DSA-4158"},{"url":"https://security.gentoo.org/glsa/201811-21","tags":["vendor-advisory","x_refsource_GENTOO"],"name":"GLSA-201811-21"},{"url":"https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHSA-2019:0367","tags":["vendor-advisory","x_refsource_REDHAT"],"name":"RHSA-2019:0367"},{"url":"https://www.debian.org/security/2018/dsa-4157","tags":["vendor-advisory","x_refsource_DEBIAN"],"name":"DSA-4157"},{"url":"https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHSA-2018:3505","tags":["vendor-advisory","x_refsource_REDHAT"],"name":"RHSA-2018:3505"},{"url":"http://www.securityfocus.com/bid/103518","tags":["vdb-entry","x_refsource_BID"],"name":"103518"},{"url":"http://www.securitytracker.com/id/1040576","tags":["vdb-entry","x_refsource_SECTRACK"],"